    ],
    "word_count": 50,
    "html": "<p>Anar Mammadov is an Azerbaijani businessman and former chairman of ZQAN, later renamed Garant Holding, whose interests spanned construction, transport, finance and hotels. The son of former transport minister Ziya Mammadov, he studied business administration in London. His British investments have included property and controlling stakes in KFC restaurant operators.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "As president of the Azerbaijan Golf Federation, businessman Anar Mammadov helped introduce the sport to a new national audience. His National Azerbaijan Golf Club in Quba hosted an international amateur tournament before welcoming the country’s first professional event. The federation hoped eventually to stage a European Tour tournament, while recognising that a local playing community needed time to develop.\n\nIts junior programme combined school visits with a practice academy in Baku. At one school, weekly lessons became part of the curriculum. Federation staff introduced children to the game, while the academy gave young players somewhere to continue practising outside those introductory sessions.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 93,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Tournament organiser documents presidency, course ownership and programme; staff work is not attributed to him personally."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S5"
    ],
    "word_count": 102,
    "html": "<p>As president of the Azerbaijan Golf Federation, businessman Anar Mammadov helped introduce the sport to a new national audience. His National Azerbaijan Golf Club in Quba hosted an international amateur tournament before welcoming the country’s first professional event. The federation hoped eventually to stage a European Tour tournament, while recognising that a local playing community needed time to develop.</p>\r\n<p>Its junior programme combined school visits with a practice academy in Baku. At one school, weekly lessons became part of the curriculum. Federation staff introduced children to the game, while the academy gave young players somewhere to continue practising outside those introductory sessions.</p>\r\n"
  },
